WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.032 --> 00:00:07.862
Sixty years ago much of Europe lay in ruins as a result of World War II, which was fought mostly on European soil.

2
00:00:07.863 --> 00:00:13.914
This conflict bitterly divided Europe and killed 55,000,000 soldiers and civilians.

3
00:00:13.915 --> 00:00:17.676
Every country on the continent was forced to choose a side.

4
00:00:17.677 --> 00:00:23.797
After the war many thought that the Europeans would never be able to agree on any political matter.

5
00:00:23.798 --> 00:00:31.003
Over the previous two thousand years, the European continent had been the scene of hundreds of battles, invasions, and wars.

6
00:00:31.004 --> 00:00:36.557
France and England were often at war with one another, as were England and Spain.

7
00:00:36.558 --> 00:00:40.609
Poland had been invaded 17 times throughout its history.

8
00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:48.370
European relations were put under even greater strain by the two World Wars, which were only 25 years apart.

9
00:00:48.371 --> 00:00:56.147
So, given Europe's long history of internal struggles, the lack of trust between nations was not surprising.

10
00:00:56.148 --> 00:01:05.087
The Second World War in particular gave expression to age-old prejudices, and could have made a lasting peace in Europe impossible.

11
00:01:05.088 --> 00:01:19.968
But, against all expectations, European countries were able to overcome their cultural differences; the leaders of Western Europe had decided it was time for their countries to cooperate economically and coexist in peace.

12
00:01:19.969 --> 00:01:32.790
The first step in achieving this goal was the formation of the European Coal and Steel Community in 1952, which brought together Belgium, France, Italy, West Germany, Luxembourg, and the Netherlands.

13
00:01:32.791 --> 00:01:41.220
Because these six countries pooled their supplies of steel, they were economically and politically unable to wage war against one another.

14
00:01:41.221 --> 00:01:51.534
In 1958 these countries went on to create the European Economic Community (EEC), allowing goods, people, and capital to move freely between them.

15
00:01:51.535 --> 00:02:01.598
Over time many other countries joined the EEC, and the organization tackled an increasing number of political issues, like security and social welfare.

16
00:02:01.599 --> 00:02:08.201
In 1993 the participating countries officially formed the European Union, or EU.

17
00:02:08.202 --> 00:02:12.787
This act made war between European countries unthinkable.

18
00:02:12.788 --> 00:02:22.671
The EU is a group of 25 European countries, known as member states, that have agreed to adhere to some of the same laws and policies.

19
00:02:22.672 --> 00:02:27.834
Reaching a compromise regarding which ones to follow was not an easy task.

20
00:02:27.835 --> 00:02:36.066
Nevertheless, after much negotiation, the countries decided to cooperate and work toward a better future for all Europeans.

21
00:02:36.067 --> 00:02:42.327
Their success has caused many other nations on the continent to become anxious to join the EU.

22
00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:49.207
Each European nation that hopes to join the Union must meet a set of criteria before they are accepted.

23
00:02:49.208 --> 00:02:56.421
These requirements are that the country must be a democracy, protect human rights, and have a market economy.

24
00:02:56.422 --> 00:03:07.297
If it does not meet the standards set by the EU, then the country must become democratic, improve how it treats its citizens, and strengthen its economy before reapplying.

25
00:03:07.298 --> 00:03:12.684
If it has made sufficient improvement, it will be welcomed into the European Union.

26
00:03:12.685 --> 00:03:17.498
The EU is not a single country, but it resembles one in some ways.

27
00:03:17.498 --> 00:03:23.185
Like all nations, the EU has its own flag, anthem, currency, and civil service.

28
00:03:23.186 --> 00:03:26.851
However, all EU passports do not look the same.

29
00:03:26.852 --> 00:03:36.770
Citizens of EU member states have similar burgundy passports with the words "European Union" on the covers, but with the names and emblems of their own nations.

30
00:03:36.771 --> 00:03:44.124
The EU is also similar to a country because it has its own institutions, including a government, a bank, and courts.

31
00:03:44.125 --> 00:03:49.597
However, the individual member states can choose not to adopt specific policies.

32
00:03:49.598 --> 00:03:59.688
The people of each nation ensure that their wishes are respected when they, or their representatives in government, democratically elect people to serve in these institutions.

33
00:03:59.689 --> 00:04:08.759
The EU is mainly governed by three institutions: the European Commission, the European Parliament, and the Council of the European Union.

34
00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:15.687
The European Commission, the executive branch of the government, has 25 members, one from each state.

35
00:04:15.688 --> 00:04:20.961
Its duties include introducing new legislation and implementing existing laws.

36
00:04:20.962 --> 00:04:25.445
The European Parliament is one of the legislative bodies of the EU.

37
00:04:25.446 --> 00:04:31.555
It has representatives from all EU member states, for a total of 732 members.

38
00:04:31.556 --> 00:04:37.616
The Parliament vigorously debates and writes the laws, as well as controls the EU's budget.

39
00:04:37.617 --> 00:04:43.277
The Council of the European Union is the other legislative body of the EU.

40
00:04:43.278 --> 00:04:52.055
The Council is made up of ministers from the governments of EU countries, who attend meetings to discuss topics in their areas of specialization.

41
00:04:52.056 --> 00:05:00.374
For example, if the EU is having a meeting about public health care, then the ministers of health from every EU country will attend.

42
00:05:00.375 --> 00:05:05.704
All EU institutions use the 21 official languages of the EU.

43
00:05:05.705 --> 00:05:12.743
This means that many materials used in the EU must be translated or interpreted into twenty other languages.

44
00:05:12.744 --> 00:05:22.078
When the EU holds meetings, everyone who attends wears headphones and listens to interpreters so that they can understand everything that is being said.

45
00:05:22.079 --> 00:05:33.337
It is often unnecessary, however, because EU policy encourages Europeans to be multilingual and speak at least two other languages in addition to their mother tongue.

46
00:05:33.338 --> 00:05:35.726
How has the EU changed Europe?

47
00:05:35.727 --> 00:05:44.778
First of all the fact that many European countries now use the same currency, the euro, makes life in Europe much more convenient and affordable.

48
00:05:44.779 --> 00:05:53.224
For instance, travel from one European country to another is less expensive because people usually don't need to exchange currencies anymore.

49
00:05:53.225 --> 00:05:59.055
Furthermore phone calls, Internet access, and air travel within Europe have become cheaper.

50
00:05:59.056 --> 00:06:03.870
Most importantly EU citizens can move to another EU country if they wish.

51
00:06:03.871 --> 00:06:12.982
This means that a Greek citizen can easily apply for a job in Spain and immigrate there without needing a visa or having to go through a lot of red tape.

52
00:06:12.983 --> 00:06:18.472
The EU has not only affected European citizens, but also citizens worldwide.

53
00:06:18.473 --> 00:06:30.365
If a group of Americans, for instance, wants to travel to Europe, no matter how many countries they visit, their passports will be stamped only twice─once when they enter Europe, and once when they leave.

54
00:06:30.366 --> 00:06:33.973
But the EU has also influenced the world as a whole.

55
00:06:33.974 --> 00:06:41.336
Because the EU is a large group of powerful, wealthy countries, its decisions can have a considerable impact.

56
00:06:41.337 --> 00:06:50.633
The EU recently banned certain toxic chemicals that are frequently used when making common household goods, like computers, refrigerators, and cosmetics.

57
00:06:50.634 --> 00:07:00.230
As a result companies all around the world must now change their policies and make their products more "green" in order to keep the valuable European market.

58
00:07:00.231 --> 00:07:07.382
Many countries have also been following the EU's example on issues affecting the whole globe, such as global warming.

59
00:07:07.383 --> 00:07:19.368
The EU is the world's biggest supporter of the Kyoto Protocol, an international agreement to cut emissions of carbon dioxide, and it has encouraged the rest of the world to sign on to the plan.

60
00:07:19.369 --> 00:07:23.370
However, some issues remain problematic within the EU.

61
00:07:23.371 --> 00:07:33.027
Each European country has a unique identity and culture, and each one's citizens remain concerned that they might lose these under a unified government.

62
00:07:33.028 --> 00:07:42.624
In 2002 a survey attempted to determine where Europeans' loyalties lie by asking them the question, "How proud are you to be European?"

63
00:07:42.625 --> 00:07:49.059
This survey found that 25 percent of people in the EU were not proud to be Europeans.

64
00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:53.060
They were, however, intensely proud of their national heritage.

65
00:07:53.061 --> 00:08:01.620
Another controversial issue is how much control can be given to the EU without endangering the independence of the member states.

66
00:08:01.621 --> 00:08:12.451
Even citizens of prominent member states, like France and the Netherlands, said that they are afraid their country might not be able to maintain its individuality within the EU.

67
00:08:12.452 --> 00:08:21.041
As a result, when the EU tried to approve its new constitution in 2004, those countries voted against it.

68
00:08:21.042 --> 00:08:26.962
By working together, the countries of the European Union have accomplished many goals.

69
00:08:26.963 --> 00:08:32.807
Former enemies now keep borders open and support peace and prosperity in all of Europe.

70
00:08:32.808 --> 00:08:38.307
The economy has improved and the standard of living for its citizens has increased.

71
00:08:38.308 --> 00:08:53.517
But what makes the EU so successful is that it makes room for differences to shine through and for cultures to maintain their unique strengths. For the most part, the motto of the European Union, "United in Diversity," rings true.

